92186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407582 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407583 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407585 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161813 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162021 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162855 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162908 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162909 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162910 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162911 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1045 "The Spectral Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162855 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049855 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162222 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407581 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94768" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162652 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162908 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1061 30533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163230 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162909 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162908 #edb/ref 1061 30970 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162019 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162909 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162911 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162909 #edb/ref 1061 30971 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163249 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162910 #edb/ref 1061 30972 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162020 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162911 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162910 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162911 #edb/ref 1061 30973 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162021 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1065 4882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1064 "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$A=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$A =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1065 4973 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1067 "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407586 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407587 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407588 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407589 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407590 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1045 "The Spectral Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186407587 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407551 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17